<!--
  VINTF device manifest for the bare-metal x86_64 PC target.

  This is intentionally empty.

  Modern AIDL HAL services declare themselves through their own vintf_fragment,
  installed to /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/. Declaring the same HAL here as well
  produces a conflict, because a bare <fqname> with no <version> defaults to
  version 1 while the module's fragment carries its real version:

    HAL "android.hardware.health" has a conflict:
      IHealth/default (@1) from /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml
      vs
      IHealth/default (@5) from .../android.hardware.health-service.example.xml

  So only add entries here for HALs that do NOT ship their own fragment, and
  always with an explicit <version>.

  Only HALs this device actually provides may be listed. Declaring one that is
  absent is worse than omitting it -- the framework will call into it and fail.
  See doc/07-hals.md.

  target-level is the Shipping FCM Version and is REQUIRED even when the
  manifest declares no HALs. Without it checkvintf --check-compat fails with:
      ERROR: Invalid argument: Device manifest does not specify Shipping FCM Version.

  202604 matches device/google/cuttlefish/shared/config/manifest.xml, the
  current reference device in this tree, and there is a corresponding
  system/etc/vintf/compatibility_matrix.202604.xml.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="202604">

</manifest>
